.Sh INSTRUCTIONS
The json code is made using instructions. Here is an example of every instruction type:
.Bl -tag -offset indent
.It {"type":"speak", "value":"welcome to the game"}
Send a message to the player using text to speech.
.It {"type":"choice", "true": [...], "false": [...]}
Let the player pick a course of action using their headphone buttons. Flow either goes to true or false, and then later returns. Often paired with a speak instruction.
.It {"type":"money", value:13}
Give the player a certain amount of currency (or, if it's negative, take it).
.It {"type":"additem", "value":"raspberries"}
Add an item to the player's backpack.
.It {"type":"delitem", "value":"raspberries", "true": [...], "false": [...]}
Attempt to remove an item from the player's backpack. It it fails, flow goes to false. It if succeeds, flow goes to true. Either way it returns later.
.It {"type":"random", "chance": 3, "true": [...], "false": [...]}
A random path is taken. As chance increases, true becomes less likely and false more likely. In this example: [chance = 3] -> [true = 1/3], [false = 2/3]
.It {"type":"inventory"}
Tell the player what's in their backpack and how much currency they have.
.It {"type":"setstage", "value": 2}
Set which stage to go to next. If it's inside a conditional instruction, this can be useful for going to different stages depending on player input or otherwise.
.El

init.json example:

{"money": 12,
 "backpack": [ "water", "compass" ],
 "value": [ {"type":"speak", "value":"hello"}, ... ]}

stage/*.json example:

{"value": [ {"type":"speak", "value":"Welcome to fluffy fields. Would you like to explore the field, or continue?"}, ... ]}

